1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,283
Here's a brand-new computer in the cloud.

2
00:00:02,603 --> 00:00:05,803
It's ours, and we have root,
full administrator rights.

3
00:00:06,123 --> 00:00:09,600
We ask pip for two Python packages,
and pip says no.

4
00:00:10,083 --> 00:00:14,648
This command isn't from the README;
it's the one most of us would type first.

5
00:00:14,968 --> 00:00:18,373
The error says
the environment is externally managed,

6
00:00:18,373 --> 00:00:20,622
and suggests a virtual environment.

7
00:00:20,942 --> 00:00:24,163
By the end of this video,
every line of it will make sense.

8
00:00:24,900 --> 00:00:28,015
Today's lecture uses the word virtual twice.

9
00:00:28,335 --> 00:00:32,132
A virtual machine is a whole computer
you rent and throw away.

10
00:00:32,452 --> 00:00:37,529
A virtual environment is a private set
of Python packages for one project.

11
00:00:37,849 --> 00:00:39,727
And one will live inside the other.

12
00:00:39,967 --> 00:00:44,295
The tutorial, simple-translate,
runs one small app four ways,

13
00:00:44,295 --> 00:00:46,708
each removing a kind of fragility.

14
00:00:47,028 --> 00:00:49,713
Today we climb the first two rungs:

15
00:00:49,713 --> 00:00:55,391
a manual install on a cloud VM,
then a fresh VM of the same kind, with uv.

16
00:00:55,711 --> 00:00:57,439
Docker comes next lecture.

17
00:00:57,683 --> 00:01:00,926
To follow along,
open the README beside this video.

18
00:01:01,246 --> 00:01:03,976
The tracker,
top left, shows the README step.

19
00:01:04,296 --> 00:01:06,814
Code panels show the file and line numbers.

20
00:01:07,134 --> 00:01:12,040
When the yellow your-turn tag appears,
pause and run the command on your own VM.

21
00:01:12,360 --> 00:01:16,181
A grey extra tag marks a command
that isn't in the README;

22
00:01:16,181 --> 00:01:18,355
extras are safe, so run them too.

23
00:01:18,675 --> 00:01:23,326
Every command is also in the study guide,
in the MLOps Learning repo.

24
00:01:23,567 --> 00:01:25,880
Before you start, finish Tutorial 0,

25
00:01:25,880 --> 00:01:29,156
so you have a Google Cloud
project with billing on.

26
00:01:29,476 --> 00:01:33,380
No service account or keys today;
all you need is a browser.

27
00:01:34,417 --> 00:01:37,596
Before we install anything,
let's see what we're installing.

28
00:01:37,916 --> 00:01:42,481
Open cli.py on GitHub;
you'll clone it onto your VM soon.

29
00:01:42,801 --> 00:01:47,068
The first two imports, argparse and asyncio,
come with Python.

30
00:01:47,388 --> 00:01:50,033
The next two, art and googletrans, don't.

31
00:01:50,353 --> 00:01:51,953
They have to come from somewhere.

32
00:01:52,273 --> 00:01:55,004
This whole lecture
is about where they come from.

33
00:01:55,250 --> 00:01:59,695
The main function prints a banner
that says AC215 in ASCII art,

34
00:01:59,695 --> 00:02:03,506
then the parsed arguments,
then the text to translate.

35
00:02:03,826 --> 00:02:08,008
If you pass a file with dash f,
the text comes from that file instead.

36
00:02:08,250 --> 00:02:11,416
At the bottom,
argparse defines four options,

37
00:02:11,416 --> 00:02:13,316
and each one has a default:

38
00:02:13,316 --> 00:02:14,936
the text I love cheese,

39
00:02:14,936 --> 00:02:18,106
source language English,
destination Italian.

40
00:02:18,426 --> 00:02:24,485
So running cli.py with no options
translates I love cheese into Italian.

41
00:02:24,734 --> 00:02:27,980
The last line starts
an event loop and runs main,

42
00:02:27,980 --> 00:02:31,027
because googletrans
version 4 is asynchronous.

43
00:02:31,347 --> 00:02:35,827
Inside main, a Translator sends
your text off with one awaited call.

44
00:02:36,067 --> 00:02:40,271
That call is a single
HTTPS request from the VM

45
00:02:40,271 --> 00:02:43,491
to translate dot googleapis dot com.

46
00:02:43,811 --> 00:02:49,763
It's Google's unofficial translate endpoint:
free, no API key, no promises.

47
00:02:50,083 --> 00:02:54,584
A little JSON comes back,
and line 39 prints the translation.

48
00:02:54,834 --> 00:02:59,869
One detail on line 25 matters later:
raise exception equals True.

49
00:03:00,189 --> 00:03:03,063
Without it,
when Google answers with an error,

50
00:03:03,063 --> 00:03:04,313
googletrans hides it

51
00:03:04,313 --> 00:03:07,371
and hands your own input back
as the translation,

52
00:03:07,371 --> 00:03:08,807
so nothing looks wrong.

53
00:03:09,127 --> 00:03:11,986
With it,
a failure becomes an error you can read.

54
00:03:12,234 --> 00:03:15,424
Lines 32 to 37 catch any error
from that call

55
00:03:15,424 --> 00:03:19,253
and wrap it in a friendlier
message about the network.

56
00:03:19,573 --> 00:03:20,895
Remember these lines.

57
00:03:21,215 --> 00:03:23,092
In a few minutes, they'll fool us.

58
00:03:23,400 --> 00:03:27,837
So the app is small:
arguments in, one web request, one line out.

59
00:03:28,157 --> 00:03:30,781
What it needs is two third-party packages.

60
00:03:31,101 --> 00:03:33,576
Let's get a computer, and find them a home.

61
00:03:34,817 --> 00:03:37,784
A virtual machine
is a slice of a real server

62
00:03:37,784 --> 00:03:39,894
in one of Google's data centers.

63
00:03:40,214 --> 00:03:44,225
Software called a hypervisor
carves the machine into slices.

64
00:03:44,545 --> 00:03:48,310
Each slice gets virtual CPUs
and memory from that server,

65
00:03:48,310 --> 00:03:52,737
a disk from Google's storage network,
and its own operating system.

66
00:03:53,057 --> 00:03:54,636
One of them will be yours.

67
00:03:54,884 --> 00:03:59,719
In the console, check
that your Tutorial 0 project is selected,

68
00:03:59,719 --> 00:04:03,097
then open Compute Engine, then VM instances.

69
00:04:03,417 --> 00:04:07,666
The first time, it may ask you
to enable the Compute Engine API;

70
00:04:07,666 --> 00:04:09,924
click enable and give it a minute.

71
00:04:10,244 --> 00:04:11,844
Then click Create instance.

72
00:04:12,084 --> 00:04:15,455
The README gives no settings,
so here are sensible ones.

73
00:04:15,775 --> 00:04:18,676
A name, like ac215-l01.

74
00:04:18,996 --> 00:04:22,196
A region and zone:
where the machine physically lives.

75
00:04:22,516 --> 00:04:26,040
A machine type,
which sets the CPUs and memory;

76
00:04:26,040 --> 00:04:27,465
e2-small is plenty.

77
00:04:27,785 --> 00:04:29,897
And a boot disk with Debian 12.

78
00:04:30,217 --> 00:04:33,801
Debian 13 works too;
we'll see where it changes things.

79
00:04:34,050 --> 00:04:37,378
Keep the external IP address;
it's on by default.

80
00:04:37,698 --> 00:04:40,464
Without it, and without extra network setup,

81
00:04:40,464 --> 00:04:44,802
this VM can't reach the internet,
and every download here would hang.

82
00:04:45,050 --> 00:04:46,607
And now the meter starts.

83
00:04:46,927 --> 00:04:52,858
CPU and memory are billed by the second
while the VM runs, even when it sits idle.

84
00:04:53,178 --> 00:04:55,631
The disk is billed for as long as it exists.

85
00:04:55,951 --> 00:04:59,578
The estimate is small,
but the meter never stops on its own.

86
00:04:59,834 --> 00:05:03,418
Click Create, wait for the green check,
then click SSH.

87
00:05:03,738 --> 00:05:07,215
If it asks you to authorize the connection,
click Authorize.

88
00:05:07,535 --> 00:05:11,375
A terminal opens in your browser,
logged in to your new VM.

89
00:05:11,695 --> 00:05:14,981
Our prompt says student;
yours shows your own username.

90
00:05:15,301 --> 00:05:17,796
Pause here, and get your VM to this point.

91
00:05:18,050 --> 00:05:20,823
Three quick extra checks, not in the README.

92
00:05:21,143 --> 00:05:23,789
First, which operating system did we get?

93
00:05:24,109 --> 00:05:28,120
Print the os-release file:
Debian 12, nicknamed bookworm.

94
00:05:28,384 --> 00:05:30,987
Second, ask python3 for its version.

95
00:05:31,307 --> 00:05:36,960
Debian 12 ships Python 3.11.2,
for the operating system itself.

96
00:05:37,280 --> 00:05:40,992
On Debian 13, you'd see 3.13.5.

97
00:05:41,312 --> 00:05:42,976
Keep both numbers in mind.

98
00:05:43,234 --> 00:05:45,623
Third, which python, without the 3.

99
00:05:45,943 --> 00:05:48,930
The which command asks
which program a name would run.

100
00:05:49,250 --> 00:05:53,943
For python, nothing comes back:
Debian has python3, but no python.

101
00:05:54,263 --> 00:05:55,970
Remember this for part 2.

102
00:05:57,033 --> 00:05:59,550
Step 3: type sudo apt-get update.

103
00:05:59,870 --> 00:06:04,436
Debian's package manager is apt,
and sudo runs it as the administrator.

104
00:06:04,756 --> 00:06:07,100
Update downloads the package catalog:

105
00:06:07,100 --> 00:06:10,836
the list of packages that exist,
and their latest versions.

106
00:06:11,156 --> 00:06:12,820
Nothing is installed yet.

107
00:06:13,140 --> 00:06:18,046
On a Google VM, you'll see a few more lines,
for Google's own package sources.

108
00:06:18,366 --> 00:06:19,518
Pause and run it.

109
00:06:19,766 --> 00:06:23,670
The README has a tip here:
apt-get and apt do the same job.

110
00:06:23,990 --> 00:06:27,325
The apt-get command is the stable,
scriptable one,

111
00:06:27,325 --> 00:06:29,793
for Dockerfiles and automated builds.

112
00:06:30,113 --> 00:06:33,035
The shorter apt is friendlier
for typing by hand.

113
00:06:33,355 --> 00:06:34,678
Either is fine here.

114
00:06:34,916 --> 00:06:37,305
Step 4: sudo apt install git.

115
00:06:37,625 --> 00:06:39,844
This time apt really installs things.

116
00:06:40,164 --> 00:06:43,556
It lists what it will add and asks,
do you want to continue?

117
00:06:43,876 --> 00:06:45,455
Type Y and press enter.

118
00:06:45,775 --> 00:06:50,020
The list depends on your VM;
if git is already there, apt says so.

119
00:06:50,340 --> 00:06:51,492
Pause and run it.

120
00:06:51,733 --> 00:06:53,610
Step 5: clone the repo.

121
00:06:53,930 --> 00:06:57,322
Type git clone, then the address;
copy it from the README.

122
00:06:57,642 --> 00:07:00,585
Git copies the whole repo, with its history,

123
00:07:00,585 --> 00:07:04,597
into a new folder, simple-translate,
in your home directory.

124
00:07:04,833 --> 00:07:07,244
Step 6: cd simple-translate.

125
00:07:07,564 --> 00:07:11,745
Watch the prompt: after the colon,
it now shows the folder you're in.

126
00:07:12,065 --> 00:07:14,604
The wavy tilde means your home folder.

127
00:07:14,924 --> 00:07:16,630
Pause, and catch up to here.

128
00:07:16,866 --> 00:07:19,575
One extra check: ls lists the files.

129
00:07:19,895 --> 00:07:21,041
There's cli.py,

130
00:07:21,041 --> 00:07:24,553
the README,
a Dockerfile saved as a text file,

131
00:07:24,553 --> 00:07:26,731
two scripts for next lecture,

132
00:07:26,731 --> 00:07:30,562
and pyproject.toml,
the list of what the app needs.

133
00:07:30,882 --> 00:07:36,386
Notice what's missing: no requirements file,
and no lock file with exact versions.

134
00:07:36,706 --> 00:07:37,922
Hold on to that.

135
00:07:39,167 --> 00:07:42,324
Step 7: sudo apt install python3-pip.

136
00:07:42,644 --> 00:07:46,570
That's Debian's own packaged copy of pip,
for Debian's Python.

137
00:07:46,890 --> 00:07:48,170
Answer Y again.

138
00:07:48,490 --> 00:07:53,375
Ours pulled in over a hundred packages;
a real VM usually needs fewer.

139
00:07:53,695 --> 00:07:54,847
Pause and run it.

140
00:07:55,084 --> 00:07:58,135
An extra check: ask pip3 for its version.

141
00:07:58,455 --> 00:08:01,679
It's pip 23.0.1, for Python 3.11,

142
00:08:01,679 --> 00:08:06,369
in a folder called dist-packages
under user lib.

143
00:08:06,689 --> 00:08:11,297
That user is spelled U-S-R:
the system's files, not your home folder.

144
00:08:11,534 --> 00:08:15,939
Now the command from the start of the video,
without sudo or any flag;

145
00:08:15,939 --> 00:08:17,827
it isn't in the README either.

146
00:08:18,147 --> 00:08:22,371
Again, pip refuses:
this environment is externally managed.

147
00:08:22,691 --> 00:08:26,851
The hint says: install with apt,
or create a virtual environment.

148
00:08:27,171 --> 00:08:29,966
Its last note even names a way around it.

149
00:08:30,200 --> 00:08:31,267
Why refuse?

150
00:08:31,587 --> 00:08:33,805
Picture how Python finds an import.

151
00:08:34,125 --> 00:08:37,787
This extra one-liner prints
the list called sys dot path:

152
00:08:37,787 --> 00:08:39,587
the folders Python searches.

153
00:08:39,907 --> 00:08:41,336
Think of them as shelves.

154
00:08:41,656 --> 00:08:45,097
On every import,
Python scans from the top shelf down,

155
00:08:45,097 --> 00:08:46,755
and takes the first match.

156
00:08:47,967 --> 00:08:49,930
The bottom shelf belongs to apt.

157
00:08:50,250 --> 00:08:54,917
Debian itself runs Python tools
that depend on exactly those jars,

158
00:08:54,917 --> 00:08:56,756
at exactly those versions.

159
00:08:57,076 --> 00:09:00,831
The system Python is a shared kitchen,
and apt is the cook.

160
00:09:01,151 --> 00:09:03,242
That's what externally managed means.

161
00:09:03,562 --> 00:09:07,082
PEP 668 is the rule
that makes pip respect it.

162
00:09:07,317 --> 00:09:09,624
Step 8 is the README's command:

163
00:09:09,624 --> 00:09:13,717
sudo pip3 install,
with the flag break-system-packages.

164
00:09:14,037 --> 00:09:18,453
The flag overrides the guard;
sudo lets pip write as administrator.

165
00:09:18,773 --> 00:09:19,925
Pause and run it.

166
00:09:20,245 --> 00:09:22,670
We asked for 2 packages and got 12;

167
00:09:22,670 --> 00:09:27,520
if yours says requirement already satisfied,
apt already had that one.

168
00:09:27,840 --> 00:09:32,746
And the warning says running pip as root
can clash with the system's package manager.

169
00:09:32,984 --> 00:09:34,115
Where did they go?

170
00:09:34,435 --> 00:09:38,142
This extra one-liner asks googletrans
where it lives:

171
00:09:38,142 --> 00:09:43,459
in dist-packages under user local,
still the system's, shared by every user.

172
00:09:43,779 --> 00:09:47,256
Look at the shelves:
that shelf sits above the one apt owns.

173
00:09:47,500 --> 00:09:52,556
Now suppose apt had installed an older httpx
for some system tool.

174
00:09:52,876 --> 00:09:55,965
Our googletrans needs 0.27.2 or newer,

175
00:09:55,965 --> 00:10:00,193
so pip would add a second copy,
on the higher shelf.

176
00:10:00,513 --> 00:10:04,469
Python would find that copy first
and hide the one from apt,

177
00:10:04,469 --> 00:10:08,492
for every program
that uses the system Python, not just ours.

178
00:10:08,812 --> 00:10:10,327
That's what the flag breaks.

179
00:10:10,567 --> 00:10:14,190
To be fair, the README
says it uses the flag deliberately,

180
00:10:14,190 --> 00:10:18,439
as a quick demonstration,
and the next walkthrough does it properly.

181
00:10:18,684 --> 00:10:20,028
Something to ponder.

182
00:10:20,348 --> 00:10:26,526
A classmate's project
on this VM needs httpx 0.13,

183
00:10:26,526 --> 00:10:29,863
and yours needs httpx 0.28.

184
00:10:30,183 --> 00:10:32,423
Both install system-wide, like this.

185
00:10:32,743 --> 00:10:33,745
What happens?

186
00:10:42,584 --> 00:10:46,808
There's only one shelf,
so there's room for only one httpx.

187
00:10:47,128 --> 00:10:51,608
The last install wins, for everyone,
and one of the two projects breaks.

188
00:10:53,150 --> 00:10:54,601
Step 9: run it.

189
00:10:54,921 --> 00:11:00,809
Type python3 cli.py, with the 3,
since plain python doesn't exist here.

190
00:11:01,129 --> 00:11:04,378
You should see the banner,
the arguments, your input,

191
00:11:04,378 --> 00:11:06,953
and the output: I love cheese, in Italian.

192
00:11:07,273 --> 00:11:10,734
If you get a long red error instead,
don't change anything;

193
00:11:10,734 --> 00:11:12,905
we'll read ours together in a minute.

194
00:11:13,225 --> 00:11:14,419
Pause and try it.

195
00:11:14,667 --> 00:11:16,608
Then the README's other two runs.

196
00:11:16,928 --> 00:11:20,479
Dash t gives the text,
dash s the source language,

197
00:11:20,479 --> 00:11:22,112
dash d the destination.

198
00:11:22,432 --> 00:11:26,443
Copy them from the README, quotes included,
and pause to run both.

199
00:11:26,763 --> 00:11:29,557
One comes back in Spanish,
the other in French.

200
00:11:29,800 --> 00:11:34,252
Now, honestly: when we recorded this,
Google refused our requests,

201
00:11:34,252 --> 00:11:37,288
and the run ended
in this long red traceback.

202
00:11:37,608 --> 00:11:40,915
Let's read it; you'll meet tracebacks
like this all course long.

203
00:11:41,150 --> 00:11:43,625
This is a chained traceback: two errors.

204
00:11:43,945 --> 00:11:47,913
The line between them says
the first one directly caused the second.

205
00:11:48,233 --> 00:11:52,222
Most people read only the last line,
but here that's just the wrapper.

206
00:11:52,542 --> 00:11:55,166
Scroll up to the first error:
that's the cause.

207
00:11:55,400 --> 00:11:59,539
The cause is status code 429:
too many requests.

208
00:11:59,859 --> 00:12:03,687
Google's unofficial endpoint
was rate-limiting our machine,

209
00:12:03,687 --> 00:12:06,088
as it often does with cloud machines.

210
00:12:06,408 --> 00:12:08,435
Nothing on the VM was broken.

211
00:12:08,683 --> 00:12:13,279
The error on the last line
is the app's own wrapper,

212
00:12:13,279 --> 00:12:15,488
lines 32 to 37 of cli.py.

213
00:12:15,808 --> 00:12:19,083
It catches anything,
and raises a friendlier message,

214
00:12:19,083 --> 00:12:20,566
chained to the original.

215
00:12:20,800 --> 00:12:22,272
Why read the first error?

216
00:12:22,592 --> 00:12:26,496
This extra command has a typo:
x x isn't a language.

217
00:12:26,816 --> 00:12:30,016
The first error says
invalid destination language.

218
00:12:30,336 --> 00:12:35,435
But the last line still blames the network,
because the same wrapper catches everything.

219
00:12:35,755 --> 00:12:38,485
A small flaw in the code, not in your setup.

220
00:12:38,805 --> 00:12:42,432
The valid codes are in a table
at the bottom of the README.

221
00:12:42,667 --> 00:12:45,329
If you hit a 429, wait and retry,

222
00:12:45,329 --> 00:12:48,960
try another region,
or run it on your laptop.

223
00:12:49,280 --> 00:12:53,430
If you got as far as the Input line,
the imports worked;

224
00:12:53,430 --> 00:12:57,579
when the first error is a 429,
only the network said no.

225
00:12:58,817 --> 00:13:01,654
Step 10, the last one: delete the VM.

226
00:13:01,974 --> 00:13:06,582
As the README says,
a running VM keeps charging, even when idle.

227
00:13:06,902 --> 00:13:09,313
So why delete it, and not just stop it?

228
00:13:09,550 --> 00:13:12,615
Stopping ends the billing
for CPU and memory,

229
00:13:12,615 --> 00:13:14,386
but the disk still exists,

230
00:13:14,386 --> 00:13:18,148
so it keeps billing,
and so would a reserved IP address.

231
00:13:18,400 --> 00:13:23,029
Deleting removes the VM,
and by default its boot disk goes with it.

232
00:13:23,349 --> 00:13:27,232
That's a setting on the boot disk,
and it's on unless you changed it.

233
00:13:27,552 --> 00:13:28,917
And the meter stops.

234
00:13:29,237 --> 00:13:32,928
Select your instance, click Delete,
and pause until it's gone.

235
00:13:33,167 --> 00:13:37,754
Then check two pages:
VM instances and Disks should both be empty.

236
00:13:38,074 --> 00:13:42,808
With the gcloud tool on your laptop,
two extra commands do the same:

237
00:13:42,808 --> 00:13:47,055
gcloud compute instances list,
and gcloud compute disks list.

238
00:13:47,375 --> 00:13:49,103
Neither is in the README.

239
00:13:49,350 --> 00:13:51,099
That was the quick and dirty way.

240
00:13:51,419 --> 00:13:55,707
It worked, but it changed the system Python
for every program on the machine.

241
00:13:56,027 --> 00:13:59,846
In part 2: the same app,
on a fresh VM, the clean way.

242
00:14:01,083 --> 00:14:02,533
Back to our puzzle:

243
00:14:02,533 --> 00:14:06,651
two projects, two versions of httpx,
one shared shelf.

244
00:14:06,971 --> 00:14:10,107
The fix is to give
each project its own toolbox.

245
00:14:10,427 --> 00:14:12,966
That toolbox is a virtual environment.

246
00:14:13,200 --> 00:14:18,768
A virtual environment is just a folder,
usually called dot venv, with three parts.

247
00:14:19,088 --> 00:14:21,776
First, a bin folder with a python inside.

248
00:14:22,096 --> 00:14:26,746
It's usually not a copy, just a link
to a real interpreter somewhere else.

249
00:14:26,983 --> 00:14:31,271
Second, its own site-packages folder,
empty when uv makes it.

250
00:14:31,591 --> 00:14:36,988
Site-packages is the usual name;
Debian calls its system ones dist-packages.

251
00:14:37,308 --> 00:14:41,255
This is where this project's packages go,
and only this project's.

252
00:14:41,500 --> 00:14:45,127
Third, a small config file, pyvenv.cfg.

253
00:14:45,447 --> 00:14:48,240
When Python starts
from the venv's bin folder,

254
00:14:48,240 --> 00:14:49,393
it reads this file.

255
00:14:49,713 --> 00:14:52,880
It says: you're in a venv,
so use my site-packages,

256
00:14:52,880 --> 00:14:54,556
and leave the system's out.

257
00:14:54,800 --> 00:14:56,613
Here's the difference on the shelves.

258
00:14:56,933 --> 00:15:00,069
Started from the venv,
Python builds a different list.

259
00:15:00,389 --> 00:15:04,699
The standard library still comes
from the real interpreter it links to.

260
00:15:05,019 --> 00:15:08,301
But both dist-packages shelves
from the system are gone,

261
00:15:08,301 --> 00:15:10,821
and the venv's own shelf takes their place.

262
00:15:11,066 --> 00:15:13,114
Same engine, different toolbox.

263
00:15:13,434 --> 00:15:15,260
And the toolboxes don't mix:

264
00:15:15,260 --> 00:15:18,910
packages in the venv
are invisible to the system Python,

265
00:15:18,910 --> 00:15:20,474
and the other way round.

266
00:15:20,716 --> 00:15:22,017
Remember pip's hint?

267
00:15:22,337 --> 00:15:26,711
It suggested python3 dash m venv,
the classic way to make one.

268
00:15:27,031 --> 00:15:29,975
Today, uv makes it for us,
and does a lot more.

269
00:15:30,216 --> 00:15:33,668
What a venv doesn't isolate:
the operating system,

270
00:15:33,668 --> 00:15:36,637
system libraries written in C, and drivers.

271
00:15:36,957 --> 00:15:42,206
Those are jobs for a whole machine,
like our VM, or a container, next lecture.

272
00:15:42,450 --> 00:15:46,930
After uv sync, we'll open the real folder
and find all three parts.

273
00:15:48,767 --> 00:15:53,610
Part 2 starts on a fresh VM,
same settings, and the meter runs again.

274
00:15:53,930 --> 00:15:57,108
Fresh matters:
this system Python never sees pip.

275
00:15:57,428 --> 00:16:00,634
Create it and SSH in; if the name is taken,

276
00:16:00,634 --> 00:16:04,511
the old VM is still being deleted,
so wait a minute.

277
00:16:04,831 --> 00:16:07,050
Pause until your terminal is open.

278
00:16:07,300 --> 00:16:12,377
Then the same four commands:
apt-get update, install git, clone, and cd.

279
00:16:12,697 --> 00:16:15,620
Pause until your prompt ends
in simple-translate.

280
00:16:15,884 --> 00:16:18,551
Step 7 installs uv in one line.

281
00:16:18,871 --> 00:16:21,093
First, curl downloads a script:

282
00:16:21,093 --> 00:16:24,750
it follows redirects,
stays quiet but shows errors,

283
00:16:24,750 --> 00:16:29,921
and fails on an HTTP error instead
of handing an error page to the shell.

284
00:16:30,184 --> 00:16:31,908
The vertical bar is a pipe:

285
00:16:31,908 --> 00:16:37,272
it hands the script straight to the shell,
so you're running code from the internet:

286
00:16:37,272 --> 00:16:40,466
uv's official installer,
which you can read first.

287
00:16:40,717 --> 00:16:41,869
Pause and run it.

288
00:16:42,189 --> 00:16:47,293
It installs uv,
version 0.13.0 at the time of recording,

289
00:16:47,293 --> 00:16:52,216
and uvx, into dot local slash bin
in your home folder.

290
00:16:52,467 --> 00:16:54,835
Let's try uv, with an extra check.

291
00:16:55,155 --> 00:16:56,392
Command not found.

292
00:16:56,712 --> 00:16:58,291
But we just installed it.

293
00:16:58,550 --> 00:17:00,014
When you type a command,

294
00:17:00,014 --> 00:17:02,821
the shell walks a list
of folders called PATH,

295
00:17:02,821 --> 00:17:05,227
left to right, and runs the first match.

296
00:17:05,547 --> 00:17:06,656
None contains uv,

297
00:17:06,656 --> 00:17:09,921
and the shell only knows
the PATH it started with.

298
00:17:10,184 --> 00:17:13,881
The installer's last lines,
and step 8, give two options:

299
00:17:13,881 --> 00:17:16,605
reload your shell, or source the env file.

300
00:17:16,925 --> 00:17:20,991
A reload means a new SSH window,
back in your home folder,

301
00:17:20,991 --> 00:17:23,795
so you'd cd into simple-translate again.

302
00:17:24,115 --> 00:17:27,592
Sourcing is quicker:
it puts that folder first in PATH.

303
00:17:27,912 --> 00:17:29,789
Pause and run the source line.

304
00:17:30,050 --> 00:17:31,605
Then two extra checks:

305
00:17:31,605 --> 00:17:35,917
uv reports its version,
and which uv points into that folder.

306
00:17:36,184 --> 00:17:37,933
Why source, and not sh?

307
00:17:38,253 --> 00:17:41,951
Plain sh would change a child shell
that exits at once;

308
00:17:41,951 --> 00:17:43,565
source changes this one.

309
00:17:43,885 --> 00:17:48,051
The installer also made
your startup files run this env file,

310
00:17:48,051 --> 00:17:51,330
so next time you connect,
uv is found by itself.

311
00:17:51,584 --> 00:17:52,843
So what is uv?

312
00:17:53,163 --> 00:17:57,082
One fast tool that replaces several:
the venv module,

313
00:17:57,082 --> 00:18:02,037
pip, pip-tools for locking,
pyenv for installing Pythons, and pipx.

314
00:18:03,483 --> 00:18:07,322
Step 9 is uv sync,
and it reads pyproject.toml,

315
00:18:07,322 --> 00:18:09,200
so let's read it first.

316
00:18:09,520 --> 00:18:10,608
The name is app.

317
00:18:10,928 --> 00:18:18,480
Two dependencies: art, version 6.5 or newer,
and googletrans, exactly 4.0.2.

318
00:18:18,800 --> 00:18:22,470
Greater-or-equal means a range;
double equals, one version.

319
00:18:22,790 --> 00:18:26,950
Line 6 is a range again:
Python 3.13 or newer.

320
00:18:27,200 --> 00:18:28,245
Now predict.

321
00:18:28,565 --> 00:18:31,040
This VM has Python 3.11.

322
00:18:31,360 --> 00:18:34,219
The project asks for 3.13 or newer.

323
00:18:34,539 --> 00:18:36,032
What will uv sync do?

324
00:18:36,352 --> 00:18:39,786
Fail, use 3.11 anyway, or something else?

325
00:18:48,633 --> 00:18:50,340
Pause and run uv sync.

326
00:18:50,660 --> 00:18:54,906
Instead of failing,
uv downloads its own Python:

327
00:18:54,906 --> 00:18:58,532
CPython 3.15.0, at the time of recording.

328
00:18:58,852 --> 00:19:02,116
Then it creates a virtual environment
in dot venv.

329
00:19:02,436 --> 00:19:08,046
If uv can't find a pyproject.toml,
cd into simple-translate first.

330
00:19:08,283 --> 00:19:09,520
Here's what happened.

331
00:19:09,840 --> 00:19:12,379
Picture Python versions on a number line.

332
00:19:12,699 --> 00:19:16,582
The constraint lights up everything
from 3.13 onward.

333
00:19:16,902 --> 00:19:20,078
First, uv looks
for an interpreter it already has:

334
00:19:20,078 --> 00:19:22,427
its own downloads, then the system's.

335
00:19:22,747 --> 00:19:27,824
Debian's 3.11.2 lands outside the range,
so it can't be used.

336
00:19:28,066 --> 00:19:30,946
So uv downloads one. Which one?

337
00:19:31,266 --> 00:19:35,510
Not 3.13,
but the newest release in the range,

338
00:19:35,510 --> 00:19:38,647
which was 3.15.0 when we recorded.

339
00:19:38,967 --> 00:19:43,554
It lives in uv's own folder in your home,
and the venv's python links to it.

340
00:19:43,800 --> 00:19:46,488
That's the catch: a constraint is not a pin.

341
00:19:46,808 --> 00:19:52,419
Before Python 3.15 came out,
this same command gave people 3.14.

342
00:19:52,739 --> 00:19:56,386
Watch this video next year,
and you may get something newer still.

343
00:19:56,633 --> 00:19:59,321
On Debian 13, the story changes.

344
00:19:59,641 --> 00:20:04,718
Its system Python, 3.13.5,
is already inside the range.

345
00:20:05,038 --> 00:20:07,652
So uv simply uses it, with no download,

346
00:20:07,652 --> 00:20:10,734
and the venv's python links
to the system one.

347
00:20:11,054 --> 00:20:14,041
Same command,
different Debian, different Python.

348
00:20:14,283 --> 00:20:16,822
To get the same Python everywhere, pin it.

349
00:20:17,142 --> 00:20:21,245
The command uv python pin 3.13,
not in the README,

350
00:20:21,245 --> 00:20:25,020
writes a small file
called dot python version,

351
00:20:25,020 --> 00:20:28,384
and the range shrinks to the 3.13 series.

352
00:20:28,704 --> 00:20:32,116
Don't run it now:
your next uv sync would rebuild the venv,

353
00:20:32,116 --> 00:20:34,486
and the rest of the video wouldn't match.

354
00:20:34,806 --> 00:20:36,406
It's in the exercises.

355
00:20:37,650 --> 00:20:38,973
Now the packages.

356
00:20:39,293 --> 00:20:43,295
A quick puzzle:
pyproject.toml lists 2 dependencies,

357
00:20:43,295 --> 00:20:47,143
and in part 1,
pip installed 12 packages for them.

358
00:20:47,463 --> 00:20:49,127
How many will uv install?

359
00:20:55,967 --> 00:20:58,420
It's 11: one fewer than part 1.

360
00:20:58,740 --> 00:21:04,372
It resolved 13 and installed 11,
each with a plus sign and an exact version.

361
00:21:04,692 --> 00:21:08,020
So where did the other 9 come from,
and which one went missing?

362
00:21:08,267 --> 00:21:13,899
Packages have dependencies of their own;
this extra command, uv tree, draws them.

363
00:21:14,219 --> 00:21:18,709
Our googletrans needs httpx,
an HTTP library,

364
00:21:18,709 --> 00:21:23,798
which needs anyio,
certifi, httpcore, h2, and more.

365
00:21:24,118 --> 00:21:25,973
Two wishes grow into a tree.

366
00:21:26,217 --> 00:21:30,769
Resolving means choosing one exact version
for every package in the tree,

367
00:21:30,769 --> 00:21:32,702
versions that all fit together.

368
00:21:33,022 --> 00:21:39,102
Then uv writes them to a new file, uv.lock,
with download addresses and checksums.

369
00:21:39,422 --> 00:21:42,537
The pyproject.toml file is your wish list.

370
00:21:42,857 --> 00:21:45,331
The uv.lock file is the receipt.

371
00:21:45,567 --> 00:21:47,380
So why 13 resolved?

372
00:21:47,700 --> 00:21:52,159
It's 11 packages, plus the app itself,
which uv doesn't download.

373
00:21:52,479 --> 00:21:54,312
The 13th is in uv.lock,

374
00:21:54,312 --> 00:21:59,092
which covers every Python
the project allows, not just ours.

375
00:21:59,412 --> 00:22:05,556
In it, anyio needs typing extensions,
but only on Python older than 3.15.

376
00:22:05,800 --> 00:22:08,872
On our 3.15, it's skipped, so 11.

377
00:22:09,192 --> 00:22:13,843
Part 1 ran on Python 3.11,
so pip installed it: 12.

378
00:22:14,163 --> 00:22:19,603
And on Debian 13, with Python 3.13,
uv installs 12 too.

379
00:22:19,850 --> 00:22:21,621
Here's why the receipt matters.

380
00:22:21,941 --> 00:22:24,629
Two students run uv sync a week apart.

381
00:22:24,949 --> 00:22:26,206
Without a shared lock,

382
00:22:26,206 --> 00:22:30,090
each resolves fresh and may get
different versions deep in the tree.

383
00:22:30,410 --> 00:22:34,613
With the same uv.lock,
they get exactly the same packages.

384
00:22:34,850 --> 00:22:36,173
Two extra checks.

385
00:22:36,493 --> 00:22:42,125
First, ls dash a shows two new things:
the dot venv folder, and uv.lock.

386
00:22:42,445 --> 00:22:45,581
And git status says uv.lock is untracked.

387
00:22:45,901 --> 00:22:48,525
It was made here;
it didn't come with the repo.

388
00:22:48,767 --> 00:22:52,159
Now, a small mistake in the README,
with an easy fix.

389
00:22:52,479 --> 00:22:56,426
It says uv.lock gives
the next person the same versions.

390
00:22:56,746 --> 00:23:01,332
But the repo never committed one,
so each student's first sync resolves fresh.

391
00:23:01,652 --> 00:23:04,532
In your own projects, commit uv.lock.

392
00:23:04,852 --> 00:23:08,809
In automated builds,
run uv sync with the locked flag,

393
00:23:08,809 --> 00:23:11,593
which refuses a missing or stale lock.

394
00:23:11,833 --> 00:23:14,564
Last, an extra: run uv sync again.

395
00:23:14,884 --> 00:23:19,678
It checks the same packages, 11 here,
in a fraction of a millisecond,

396
00:23:19,678 --> 00:23:22,735
and changes nothing: safe to re-run anytime.

397
00:23:23,983 --> 00:23:26,138
Let's open the venv, as promised.

398
00:23:26,458 --> 00:23:29,297
This extra command lists its bin folder:

399
00:23:29,297 --> 00:23:33,839
python, a link to the Python uv downloaded,
and, notice, no pip.

400
00:23:34,783 --> 00:23:38,853
And the config file:
home is the real interpreter's folder,

401
00:23:38,853 --> 00:23:43,060
include system site packages is false,
and the prompt is app.

402
00:23:43,380 --> 00:23:45,194
All three parts, as drawn.

403
00:23:45,433 --> 00:23:49,102
Step 10:
source dot venv slash bin slash activate.

404
00:23:49,422 --> 00:23:50,574
Pause and run it.

405
00:23:50,894 --> 00:23:53,774
Your prompt now starts with app,
in parentheses.

406
00:23:54,094 --> 00:23:56,612
The README's example says simple-translate.

407
00:23:56,932 --> 00:24:02,244
But uv names the prompt after the project,
and pyproject.toml calls it app.

408
00:24:02,483 --> 00:24:03,464
What changed?

409
00:24:03,784 --> 00:24:08,243
Before activating, python knocked
on every door in PATH and found nothing.

410
00:24:08,563 --> 00:24:11,151
The activate script saves your old PATH,

411
00:24:11,151 --> 00:24:16,392
then puts the bin folder of the venv first,
so python is found at the first door.

412
00:24:16,633 --> 00:24:21,325
Extra checks:
which python points into dot venv,

413
00:24:21,325 --> 00:24:26,478
and python reports 3.15.0,
at the time of recording;

414
00:24:26,478 --> 00:24:28,558
on Debian 13, 3.13.5.

415
00:24:28,878 --> 00:24:30,521
And which pip finds nothing.

416
00:24:31,416 --> 00:24:33,967
Another small mistake in the README:

417
00:24:33,967 --> 00:24:38,925
it says activation points pip into the venv,
but a uv venv has no pip.

418
00:24:39,245 --> 00:24:43,362
On a machine with a system pip,
which pip finds that one instead,

419
00:24:43,362 --> 00:24:45,389
and it refuses, as at the start.

420
00:24:45,709 --> 00:24:48,284
To add a package, use uv add,

421
00:24:48,284 --> 00:24:52,899
which also records it
in pyproject.toml and uv.lock.

422
00:24:53,133 --> 00:24:56,526
Activation also sets
a variable called virtual env,

423
00:24:56,526 --> 00:24:57,807
changes the prompt,

424
00:24:57,807 --> 00:25:01,581
and adds a command called deactivate
that undoes it all.

425
00:25:01,901 --> 00:25:05,634
It only changes where the shell looks first,
and only in this shell.

426
00:25:05,954 --> 00:25:11,480
If your SSH window reconnects,
cd into simple-translate and activate again.

427
00:25:11,766 --> 00:25:15,542
Step 11: the same three runs,
now with plain python.

428
00:25:15,862 --> 00:25:19,162
Copy the long ones from the README,
quotes included,

429
00:25:19,162 --> 00:25:20,875
and pause to run all three.

430
00:25:21,195 --> 00:25:24,715
This time Google answered:
these are real translations.

431
00:25:25,035 --> 00:25:30,028
If you get a 429 again
but see the Input line, your venv works.

432
00:25:30,266 --> 00:25:33,594
Two extras, not in the README,
that skip activation.

433
00:25:33,914 --> 00:25:40,655
First, uv run python cli.py syncs the venv
if needed, then runs the app inside it.

434
00:25:40,975 --> 00:25:43,152
Or call the venv's python by its path.

435
00:25:43,400 --> 00:25:46,387
And why source,
not just run the activate script?

436
00:25:46,707 --> 00:25:49,815
It isn't even executable,
and run as a program,

437
00:25:49,815 --> 00:25:54,642
it would only change a child shell
that exits at once, like the env file.

438
00:25:55,883 --> 00:25:58,507
Let's prove the isolation with a few extras.

439
00:25:58,827 --> 00:26:03,798
Deactivate first: while the venv is active,
even python3 points into it.

440
00:26:04,118 --> 00:26:07,872
Then ask the system's
python3 to import googletrans.

441
00:26:08,192 --> 00:26:09,792
ModuleNotFoundError.

442
00:26:10,112 --> 00:26:13,483
On this VM,
the packages exist only in the venv.

443
00:26:13,716 --> 00:26:18,175
On the shelves: the system Python
scans its own list, top to bottom.

444
00:26:18,495 --> 00:26:22,292
The venv's shelf isn't on that list,
so the search comes up empty.

445
00:26:22,616 --> 00:26:27,245
Now the same import through uv run,
printing where this Python thinks it lives.

446
00:26:27,565 --> 00:26:30,360
It works,
and the answer is our dot venv folder.

447
00:26:30,600 --> 00:26:33,245
Now imagine you'd kept your part-1 VM.

448
00:26:33,565 --> 00:26:37,682
Same import, system python3, no venv.
Does it work?

449
00:26:44,516 --> 00:26:50,372
Yes: on that VM, sudo pip put googletrans
on the system-wide user local shelf,

450
00:26:50,372 --> 00:26:52,324
so every program finds it.

451
00:26:52,644 --> 00:26:55,460
Same import,
different shelves, different answer.

452
00:26:55,700 --> 00:26:57,449
And it works the other way too.

453
00:26:57,769 --> 00:27:03,228
Even on that messy part-1 VM, a venv
would ignore everything sudo pip put there,

454
00:27:03,228 --> 00:27:06,025
because those shelves aren't on its list.

455
00:27:06,345 --> 00:27:08,521
That's the whole lesson in one picture.

456
00:27:10,167 --> 00:27:14,668
One more tool you'll meet:
pipenv, an older way to do the same job.

457
00:27:14,988 --> 00:27:19,746
This repo's pipenv branch,
an older version of this tutorial, uses it.

458
00:27:20,066 --> 00:27:25,570
So do the separate AWS version and,
later, the cheese app's API service.

459
00:27:25,817 --> 00:27:27,737
Same ideas, different names.

460
00:27:28,057 --> 00:27:34,713
The pyproject.toml file becomes the Pipfile,
and uv.lock becomes Pipfile.lock.

461
00:27:35,033 --> 00:27:39,044
The venv lives in a folder
in your home instead of in the project.

462
00:27:39,364 --> 00:27:43,652
The command uv sync becomes pipenv install,
or pipenv sync.

463
00:27:43,972 --> 00:27:45,636
And instead of activating,

464
00:27:45,636 --> 00:27:48,644
pipenv shell opens
a new shell inside the venv.

465
00:27:48,964 --> 00:27:50,478
Type exit to leave it.

466
00:27:50,717 --> 00:27:52,978
And here's our puzzle again, for real.

467
00:27:53,298 --> 00:27:57,821
That branch's lock file pins httpx 0.13.3,

468
00:27:57,821 --> 00:28:04,392
while our uv sync installed 0.28.1,
at the time of recording.

469
00:28:04,712 --> 00:28:08,637
Two versions of the same app,
two versions of httpx.

470
00:28:08,957 --> 00:28:12,221
On one shared system Python,
they couldn't live together.

471
00:28:12,541 --> 00:28:14,013
In two venvs, they can.

472
00:28:17,050 --> 00:28:20,265
Step 12: delete the VM, with its boot disk,

473
00:28:20,265 --> 00:28:24,303
then check that VM instances
and Disks are both empty.

474
00:28:24,623 --> 00:28:27,034
Pause and do it now, before you forget.

475
00:28:27,283 --> 00:28:28,520
Here's where we are.

476
00:28:28,840 --> 00:28:32,744
The VM gave us a whole machine
that we can wreck and delete.

477
00:28:33,064 --> 00:28:37,139
The venv keeps one project's packages away
from that machine's Python.

478
00:28:37,459 --> 00:28:40,361
And between the two,
there's room for one more layer.

479
00:28:40,600 --> 00:28:43,523
That layer is a container,
and it's next lecture.

480
00:28:43,843 --> 00:28:48,314
The tutorial's Dockerfile already
shows the idea: copy the code,

481
00:28:48,314 --> 00:28:49,804
run the same uv sync,

482
00:28:49,804 --> 00:28:52,995
and start in a shell
with the venv activated.

483
00:28:53,315 --> 00:28:56,067
Everything you did today,
baked into an image.

484
00:28:56,317 --> 00:28:58,280
One thing to watch for next time.

485
00:28:58,600 --> 00:29:02,405
The Dockerfile starts
from a Python 3.12 image,

486
00:29:02,405 --> 00:29:05,320
but the project wants 3.13 or newer.

487
00:29:05,640 --> 00:29:08,135
You already know what uv will do about that.

488
00:29:08,383 --> 00:29:11,412
Before you go,
five questions to test yourself.

489
00:29:11,732 --> 00:29:15,637
One: what does the break-system-packages
flag actually break,

490
00:29:15,637 --> 00:29:18,069
and where did sudo pip3 put the files?

491
00:29:18,317 --> 00:29:21,368
Two: name the three parts
of a virtual environment.

492
00:29:21,688 --> 00:29:24,278
Three: what exactly does activation change,

493
00:29:24,278 --> 00:29:27,170
and what are two ways
to run the app without it?

494
00:29:27,417 --> 00:29:30,617
Four: 2 dependencies became 11 packages.

495
00:29:30,937 --> 00:29:33,966
Why, and which file makes
everyone get the same ones?

496
00:29:34,286 --> 00:29:37,694
Five:
is requires Python 3.13 or newer a pin,

497
00:29:37,694 --> 00:29:41,177
and what would make
the version deterministic?

498
00:29:41,417 --> 00:29:43,593
The answers are in the exercises.

499
00:29:43,913 --> 00:29:47,043
Every command from today
is in the study guide,

500
00:29:47,043 --> 00:29:48,841
in the MLOps Learning repo.

501
00:29:49,161 --> 00:29:50,867
Next time: containers.

